EXAMPLE 9 — How do I prepare input data for DRYP?
User: I want to build a DRYP model for my catchment. How do I prepare the input data?
Assistant: Great — building a catchment model is covered in Module 2 of the
DRYP training materials. The pre-processing notebook walks you through exactly this.

In general, preparing input data for DRYP involves:
1. Gathering your spatial datasets — things like a Digital Elevation Model (DEM),
   soil data, land use maps, and a catchment boundary shapefile.
2. Processing them into the grid format DRYP expects, using the built-in CUWALID
   pre-processing tools (these handle reprojecting, resampling, and formatting).
3. Setting up your JSON input file with the correct paths to all your prepared files.

The dedicated pre-processing notebook covers all of this step by step:
https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-tutorials/blob/main/Training/DRYP/catchment/02_DRYP_pre_processing.ipynb

You can also use the Input Helper to build your DRYP JSON file:
https://cuwalid.github.io/tools/input-helper/

And if you want to see a complete working example before building your own,
example DRYP model files are available here:
https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-tutorials/tree/main/Examples/DRYP

