EXAMPLE 11 — What is the DRYP model grid and how does it work?
User: I keep hearing about the "model grid" in DRYP. What does that mean?
Assistant: The model grid is how DRYP divides your study area into manageable
pieces for calculation.

Imagine taking a map of your catchment and laying a grid over it — like graph
paper placed on top. Each square in the grid is called a cell. DRYP calculates
the water balance (how much water comes in, evaporates, soaks in, runs off, etc.)
for each cell, at each time step (usually daily).

The size of the cells (called the grid resolution) affects both the accuracy
and the computation time — smaller cells give more detail but take longer to run.

This grid-based approach is what makes DRYP suitable for basin-scale modelling —
you can cover a whole river catchment, with spatial variation in soil types,
land cover, and topography captured cell by cell.

The second notebook in the DRYP training series explains this in detail with
hands-on examples:
https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-tutorials/blob/main/Training/DRYP/intro_modelling/02_DRYP_model_development.ipynb

---

EXAMPLE 12 — I want to try DRYP with a simple test before using real data
User: I don't have my own data yet. Can I still try running DRYP to see how it works?
Assistant: Absolutely — and this is actually the recommended way to start!

The DRYP training materials include synthetic (made-up) examples specifically
designed so you can learn how the model works without needing your own real data.
Notebook 3 of Module 1 is dedicated to this:

https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-tutorials/blob/main/Training/DRYP/intro_modelling/03_DRYP_test_models.ipynb

There are also ready-made example model files you can download and run directly:
https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-tutorials/tree/main/Examples/DRYP

And the full CUWALID Example repository has complete input files you can use
as a starting point:
https://github.com/AndresQuichimbo/CUWALID-Example

These are great for getting a feel for how DRYP behaves before you invest time
preparing your own catchment data.

